      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> A new Israel-Iran direct exchange (F3 residual 22%) or an Iranian Lebanon retaliation before June 19 collapses the MOU track and returns the conflict to active bilateral missile exchange.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> MOU signing occurs June 19 but implementation stalls on the Hormuz normalization clause (F4 p=8% by July 2) and the Lebanon-track dispute between Iran's MOU interpretation and Israel's operational posture, producing a frozen ceasefire without economic normalization.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> MOU signed June 19 on schedule (F8 p=72%; F2 p=85%), follow-on nuclear talks open within 60 days, and Hormuz MCM establishes a commercial lane by mid-July — translating the diplomatic framework into measurable shipping recovery.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Iranian state media framed the MOU deal as a vindication of Iran's wartime posture; Foreign Minister Araghchi described the agreement as the result of two months of negotiations consolidating the victory the Iranian people already achieved in the war; the armed forces characterized the 14-point framework — which includes a clause preserving a 440-kilogram enriched uranium stockpile during 60-day follow-on talks and carries no immediate dismantlement requirement — as evidence that the US accepted Iranian terms; IRNA published purported full deal details before Western outlets carried the announcement. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Khatam al-Anbiya Central Headquarters issued an explicit June 16 statement warning Israel that continued Lebanon attacks would draw a direct Iranian response, citing an MOU clause Iran interprets as requiring a halt to Israeli Lebanon operations — a reading both Jerusalem and Washington publicly dispute; the warning was amplified across IRGC-affiliated channels, with Press TV carrying language describing readiness to broaden operations encompassing all American-Zionist targets; Foreign Ministry and IRGC messaging ran on parallel tracks, with Araghchi telling Al Jazeera that nuclear talks begin only after MOU signing while hardliner outlets (Kayhan, Tasnim) carried IRGC deterrence language without commentary on nuclear provisions. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Supreme Leader Khamenei had not issued a public statement on the MOU as of June 17 per state media monitoring; Iran International and others noted this absence, with past IRNA precedent showing Khamenei statements on strategic agreements appearing immediately before signing to authorize or immediately after to frame; Kayhan and Tasnim compartmentalized their coverage, carrying IRGC Lebanon warnings while leaving the MOU's nuclear provisions unaddressed. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

